/*
 * content/ch1.js — Chapter 1 reader content.
 *
 * Text adapted from Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs, 2e
 * (Abelson & Sussman), Creative Commons BY-SA 4.0 unabridged edition by
 * Andres Raba. Exercises are intentionally omitted per project scope.
 *
 * Block types consumed by app.js:
 *   {t:'sec',    n, title}
 *   {t:'sub',    n, title}
 *   {t:'subsub', title}
 *   {t:'p',      html}
 *   {t:'ul'|'ol',items:[html,...]}
 *   {t:'quote',  html}                      blockquote
 *   {t:'code',   code, live?}               live=true -> runnable REPL
 *   {t:'stepper',code}                      seeds the substitution stepper
 */
window.SICP_CH1 = [
  { t: 'sec', n: '1.1', title: 'The Elements of Programming' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'A powerful programming language is more than just a means for instructing a computer to perform tasks. The language also serves as a framework within which we organize our ideas about processes. Thus, when we describe a language, we should pay particular attention to the means that the language provides for combining simple ideas to form more complex ideas. Every powerful language has three mechanisms for accomplishing this:' },
  { t: 'ul', items: [
    '<b>primitive expressions</b>, which represent the simplest entities the language is concerned with,',
    '<b>means of combination</b>, by which compound elements are built from simpler ones, and',
    '<b>means of abstraction</b>, by which compound elements can be named and manipulated as units.' ] },
  { t: 'p', html: 'In programming, we deal with two kinds of elements: procedures and data. (Later we will discover that they are really not so distinct.) Informally, data is &ldquo;stuff&rdquo; that we want to manipulate, and procedures are descriptions of the rules for manipulating the data. Thus, any powerful programming language should be able to describe primitive data and primitive procedures and should have methods for combining and abstracting procedures and data.' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'In this chapter we will deal only with simple numerical data so that we can focus on the rules for building procedures. In later chapters we will see that these same rules allow us to build procedures to manipulate compound data as well.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.1.1', title: 'Expressions' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'One easy way to get started at programming is to examine some typical interactions with an interpreter for the Scheme dialect of Lisp. Imagine that you are sitting at a computer terminal. You type an <em>expression</em>, and the interpreter responds by displaying the result of its <em>evaluating</em> that expression. One kind of primitive expression you might type is a number. If you present Lisp with a number, the interpreter will respond by printing the number. Try evaluating each line below:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '486' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Expressions representing numbers may be combined with an expression representing a primitive procedure (such as <code>+</code> or <code>*</code>) to form a compound expression that represents the application of the procedure to those numbers. For example:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(+ 137 349)\n(- 1000 334)\n(* 5 99)\n(/ 10 5)\n(+ 2.7 10)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Expressions such as these, formed by delimiting a list of expressions within parentheses in order to denote procedure application, are called <em>combinations</em>. The leftmost element in the list is called the <em>operator</em>, and the other elements are called <em>operands</em>. The value of a combination is obtained by applying the procedure specified by the operator to the <em>arguments</em> that are the values of the operands.' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The convention of placing the operator to the left of the operands is known as <em>prefix notation</em>. One advantage is that it can accommodate procedures that may take an arbitrary number of arguments:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(+ 21 35 12 7)\n(* 25 4 12)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'A second advantage of prefix notation is that it extends in a straightforward way to allow combinations to be <em>nested</em>, that is, to have combinations whose elements are themselves combinations:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(+ (* 3 5) (- 10 6))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'There is no limit (in principle) to the depth of such nesting. It is we humans who get confused by still relatively simple expressions such as the following, which the interpreter would readily evaluate to be 57:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(+ (* 3 (+ (* 2 4) (+ 3 5))) (+ (- 10 7) 6))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We can help ourselves by writing such an expression following a formatting convention known as <em>pretty-printing</em>, in which each long combination is written so that the operands are aligned vertically. The resulting indentations display clearly the structure of the expression:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(+ (* 3\n      (+ (* 2 4)\n         (+ 3 5)))\n   (+ (- 10 7)\n      6))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Even with complex expressions, the interpreter always operates in the same basic cycle: it reads an expression, evaluates it, and prints the result. This mode of operation is often expressed by saying that the interpreter runs in a <em>read-eval-print loop</em>.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.1.2', title: 'Naming and the Environment' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'A critical aspect of a programming language is the means it provides for using names to refer to computational objects. We say that the name identifies a <em>variable</em> whose <em>value</em> is the object. In the Scheme dialect of Lisp, we name things with <code>define</code>. Typing the following causes the interpreter to associate the value 2 with the name <code>size</code>:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define size 2)\nsize\n(* 5 size)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Here are further examples of the use of <code>define</code>:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define pi 3.14159)\n(define radius 10)\n(* pi (* radius radius))\n(define circumference (* 2 pi radius))\ncircumference' },
  { t: 'p', html: '<code>define</code> is our language&rsquo;s simplest means of abstraction, for it allows us to use simple names to refer to the results of compound operations. In general, computational objects may have very complex structures, and it would be extremely inconvenient to have to remember and repeat their details each time we want to use them. Indeed, complex programs are constructed by building, step by step, computational objects of increasing complexity.' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'It should be clear that the possibility of associating values with symbols and later retrieving them means that the interpreter must maintain some sort of memory that keeps track of the name-object pairs. This memory is called the <em>environment</em> (more precisely the <em>global environment</em>, since we will see later that a computation may involve a number of different environments).' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.1.3', title: 'Evaluating Combinations' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'One of our goals in this chapter is to isolate issues about thinking procedurally. As a case in point, let us consider that, in evaluating combinations, the interpreter is itself following a procedure.' },
  { t: 'quote', html: 'To evaluate a combination, do the following:<ol><li>Evaluate the subexpressions of the combination.</li><li>Apply the procedure that is the value of the leftmost subexpression (the operator) to the arguments that are the values of the other subexpressions (the operands).</li></ol>' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Even this simple rule illustrates some important points about processes in general. First, observe that in order to accomplish the evaluation process for a combination we must first perform the evaluation process on each element of the combination. Thus, the evaluation rule is <em>recursive</em> in nature; that is, it includes, as one of its steps, the need to invoke the rule itself.' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Notice how succinctly the idea of recursion can be used to express what, in the case of a deeply nested combination, would otherwise be viewed as a rather complicated process. For example, evaluating <code>(* (+ 2 (* 4 6)) (+ 3 5 7))</code> requires that the evaluation rule be applied to four different combinations. We can picture this process as a tree: each combination is a node with branches for its operator and operands; the values of the operands percolate upward, starting from the terminal nodes and combining at higher and higher levels. The &ldquo;percolate values upward&rdquo; form of the rule is an example of <em>tree accumulation</em>. Watch it happen one reduction at a time:' },
  { t: 'stepper', code: '(* (+ 2 (* 4 6)) (+ 3 5 7))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Repeated application of the first step brings us to the point where we need to evaluate primitive expressions such as numerals, built-in operators, or other names. We take care of the primitive cases by stipulating that the values of numerals are the numbers that they name; the values of built-in operators are the machine instruction sequences that carry out the corresponding operations; and the values of other names are the objects associated with those names in the environment.' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Notice that the evaluation rule does not handle definitions. Evaluating <code>(define x 3)</code> does not apply <code>define</code> to two arguments, since the purpose of <code>define</code> is precisely to associate <code>x</code> with a value. Such exceptions to the general evaluation rule are called <em>special forms</em>. Each special form has its own evaluation rule. The various kinds of expressions, each with its evaluation rule, constitute the syntax of the language.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.1.4', title: 'Compound Procedures' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We have identified some of the elements that must appear in any powerful programming language: numbers and arithmetic are primitive data and procedures; nesting of combinations provides a means of combining operations; and definitions that associate names with values provide a limited means of abstraction. Now we will learn about <em>procedure definitions</em>, a much more powerful abstraction technique by which a compound operation can be given a name and then referred to as a unit.' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We begin by examining how to express the idea of &ldquo;squaring.&rdquo; We might say, &ldquo;To square something, multiply it by itself.&rdquo; This is expressed in our language as:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (square x) (* x x))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We have here a <em>compound procedure</em>, which has been given the name <code>square</code>. The general form of a procedure definition is <code>(define (&#10216;name&#10217; &#10216;formal parameters&#10217;) &#10216;body&#10217;)</code>. Having defined <code>square</code>, we can now use it (run the block above first, then this one):' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(square 21)\n(square (+ 2 5))\n(square (square 3))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We can also use <code>square</code> as a building block in defining other procedures. For example, we can define <code>sum-of-squares</code> that, given any two numbers, produces the sum of their squares:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (sum-of-squares x y)\n  (+ (square x) (square y)))\n(sum-of-squares 3 4)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Now we can use <code>sum-of-squares</code> as a building block in constructing further procedures:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (f a)\n  (sum-of-squares (+ a 1) (* a 2)))\n(f 5)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Compound procedures are used in exactly the same way as primitive procedures. Indeed, one could not tell by looking at the definition of <code>sum-of-squares</code> whether <code>square</code> was built into the interpreter, like <code>+</code> and <code>*</code>, or defined as a compound procedure.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.1.5', title: 'The Substitution Model for Procedure Application' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'To apply a compound procedure to arguments, the interpreter follows the same process as for primitive procedures: it evaluates the elements of the combination and applies the procedure to the arguments. For compound procedures the application process is:' },
  { t: 'quote', html: 'To apply a compound procedure to arguments, evaluate the body of the procedure with each formal parameter replaced by the corresponding argument.' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'This is called the <em>substitution model</em> for procedure application. Below the stepper has the definitions of <code>square</code>, <code>sum-of-squares</code> and <code>f</code> preloaded, and the expression <code>(f 5)</code>. Press <b>Step</b> repeatedly and watch the formal parameter <code>a</code> get replaced by <code>5</code>, then the operands reduce, then <code>sum-of-squares</code> applies, and so on down to 136. This is <em>applicative order</em>: evaluate the arguments and then apply.' },
  { t: 'stepper', code: '(define (square x) (* x x))\n(define (sum-of-squares x y)\n  (+ (square x) (square y)))\n(define (f a)\n  (sum-of-squares (+ a 1) (* a 2)))\n(f 5)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Two points should be stressed. First, the purpose of substitution is to help us think about procedure application, not to describe how the interpreter really works; in practice the &ldquo;substitution&rdquo; is accomplished by using a local environment for the formal parameters (Chapter 3). Second, over the course of this book we will present a sequence of increasingly elaborate models of how interpreters work. The substitution model is only the first of these&mdash;a way to get started thinking formally about evaluation. In particular, when we address procedures with &ldquo;mutable data&rdquo; in Chapter 3, the substitution model will break down and must be replaced.' },
  { t: 'subsub', title: 'Applicative order versus normal order' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'An alternative evaluation model would not evaluate the operands until their values were needed. Instead it would first substitute operand expressions for parameters until it obtained an expression involving only primitive operators, and would then perform the evaluation. Under this method, the evaluation of <code>(f 5)</code> would first <em>fully expand</em>:' },
  { t: 'code', code: '(sum-of-squares (+ 5 1) (* 5 2))\n(+ (square (+ 5 1)) (square (* 5 2)))\n(+ (* (+ 5 1) (+ 5 1)) (* (* 5 2) (* 5 2)))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'and only <em>then reduce</em>:' },
  { t: 'code', code: '(+ (* 6 6) (* 10 10))\n(+ 36 100)\n136' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'This &ldquo;fully expand and then reduce&rdquo; method is known as <em>normal-order evaluation</em>, in contrast to the &ldquo;evaluate the arguments and then apply&rdquo; method the interpreter actually uses, called <em>applicative-order evaluation</em>. Notice that here <code>(+ 5 1)</code> and <code>(* 5 2)</code> are each evaluated twice. For procedures that can be modeled by substitution and that yield legitimate values, the two orders produce the same value; Lisp uses applicative order partly for efficiency and partly because normal order becomes much more complicated once we leave the realm of substitution.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.1.6', title: 'Conditional Expressions and Predicates' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The procedures we can define so far are limited, because we have no way to make tests and to perform different operations depending on the result of a test. For instance, we cannot yet define absolute value, which requires testing whether a number is positive, negative, or zero. This construct is called a <em>case analysis</em>, and there is a special form in Lisp for it called <code>cond</code> (for &ldquo;conditional&rdquo;):' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (abs x)\n  (cond ((> x 0) x)\n        ((= x 0) 0)\n        ((< x 0) (- x))))\n(abs -7)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The general form is <code>(cond (&#10216;p&#8321;&#10217; &#10216;e&#8321;&#10217;) (&#10216;p&#8322;&#10217; &#10216;e&#8322;&#10217;) &hellip; (&#10216;p&#8345;&#10217; &#10216;e&#8345;&#10217;))</code>, consisting of the symbol <code>cond</code> followed by parenthesized pairs of expressions called <em>clauses</em>. The first expression in each pair is a <em>predicate</em>&mdash;an expression whose value is interpreted as either true or false. Predicates are evaluated in order until one is found to be true, in which case the value of the corresponding <em>consequent expression</em> is returned. Step through it to see the false clauses get discarded:' },
  { t: 'stepper', code: '(define (abs x)\n  (cond ((> x 0) x)\n        ((= x 0) 0)\n        ((< x 0) (- x))))\n(abs -7)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Another way to write absolute value uses the special symbol <code>else</code>, which can be used in place of the predicate in the final clause:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (abs2 x)\n  (cond ((< x 0) (- x))\n        (else x)))\n(abs2 -5)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Yet another way uses the special form <code>if</code>, a restricted conditional for precisely two cases. Its general form is <code>(if &#10216;predicate&#10217; &#10216;consequent&#10217; &#10216;alternative&#10217;)</code>:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (abs3 x)\n  (if (< x 0)\n      (- x)\n      x))\n(abs3 -42)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'In addition to primitive predicates such as <code>&lt;</code>, <code>=</code>, and <code>&gt;</code>, there are logical composition operations for building compound predicates: <code>(and &#10216;e&#8321;&#10217; &hellip; &#10216;e&#8345;&#10217;)</code>, <code>(or &#10216;e&#8321;&#10217; &hellip; &#10216;e&#8345;&#10217;)</code>, and <code>(not &#10216;e&#10217;)</code>. Notice that <code>and</code> and <code>or</code> are special forms, not procedures, because their subexpressions are not necessarily all evaluated; <code>not</code> is an ordinary procedure. For example, the condition that <code>x</code> be in the range 5 &lt; x &lt; 10:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define x 7)\n(and (> x 5) (< x 10))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'And a predicate to test whether one number is greater than or equal to another:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (>= x y) (or (> x y) (= x y)))\n(>= 4 4)' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.1.7', title: 'Example: Square Roots by Newton&rsquo;s Method' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Procedures, as introduced above, are much like ordinary mathematical functions: they specify a value that is determined by one or more parameters. But there is an important difference between mathematical functions and computer procedures: procedures must be effective. The contrast is captured by the distinction between <em>declarative</em> knowledge (what is) and <em>imperative</em> knowledge (how to). Consider the square root: mathematically, &radic;x is the nonnegative y such that y&sup2; = x. This declarative definition does not describe a procedure. How does one actually compute square roots?' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The most common way is to use Newton&rsquo;s method of successive approximations: whenever we have a guess <code>y</code> for the value of the square root of a number <code>x</code>, we can perform a simple manipulation to get a better guess (one closer to the actual square root) by averaging <code>y</code> with <code>x/y</code>. We formulate the process in terms of procedures, beginning with the central idea:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (improve guess x)\n  (average guess (/ x guess)))\n(define (average x y)\n  (/ (+ x y) 2))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'A guess is improved by averaging it with the quotient of the radicand and the old guess. We also need to decide when a guess is good enough: the idea is to improve the answer until it is close enough so that its square differs from the radicand by less than a predetermined tolerance:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (good-enough? guess x)\n  (< (abs (- (square guess) x)) 0.001))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Finally, we need a way to say that the square root of <code>x</code> is the result of starting with an initial guess of 1 and improving repeatedly until the guess is good enough. The <code>sqrt-iter</code> procedure expresses this idea, and it demonstrates how recursion alone, with no special looping construct, suffices to express an iterative process:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (sqrt-iter guess x)\n  (if (good-enough? guess x)\n      guess\n      (sqrt-iter (improve guess x) x)))\n(define (my-sqrt x)\n  (sqrt-iter 1.0 x))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'With <code>abs</code> and <code>square</code> from earlier in this section also defined, run the whole machine. (If the REPL complains that <code>abs</code> or <code>square</code> is undefined, evaluate their defining blocks above first; the environment is shared down the page.)' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (square x) (* x x))\n(define (abs x) (if (< x 0) (- x) x))\n(my-sqrt 9)\n(my-sqrt (+ 100 37))\n(square (my-sqrt 1000))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Notice that the entire <code>sqrt</code> program can be viewed as a cluster of procedures that mirrors our decomposition of the problem into subproblems. This observation leads us to the central concern of the next section.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.1.8', title: 'Procedures as Black-Box Abstractions' },
  { t: 'p', html: '<code>sqrt</code> is our first example of a process defined by a set of mutually defined procedures. Notice that the definition of <code>sqrt-iter</code> is <em>recursive</em>; that is, the procedure is defined in terms of itself. The idea of being able to define a procedure in terms of itself may be disturbing; it may seem unclear how such a &ldquo;circular&rdquo; definition could make sense at all. We will examine this more carefully shortly. But first observe that the overall problem of computing square roots breaks up naturally into a number of subproblems: how to tell whether a guess is good enough, how to improve a guess, and so on. Each task is accomplished by a separate procedure. The entire program can be viewed as a cluster of procedures that mirrors the decomposition of the problem into subproblems.' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'It is crucial that each procedure accomplishes an identifiable task that can be used as a module in defining other procedures. When we define <code>good-enough?</code> in terms of <code>square</code>, we are able to regard the <code>square</code> procedure as a &ldquo;black box.&rdquo; We are not at that moment concerned with <em>how</em> the procedure computes its result, only with the fact <em>that</em> it computes the square. The details of how the square is computed can be suppressed, to be considered at a later time. Indeed, as far as <code>good-enough?</code> is concerned, <code>square</code> is not quite a procedure but rather an abstraction of a procedure, a so-called <em>procedural abstraction</em>. At this level of abstraction, any procedure that computes the square is equally good.' },
  { t: 'subsub', title: 'Local names' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'One detail of a procedure&rsquo;s implementation that should not matter to the user of the procedure is the implementer&rsquo;s choice of names for the procedure&rsquo;s formal parameters. Thus, the following two procedures should not be distinguishable:' },
  { t: 'code', code: '(define (square x) (* x x))\n(define (square y) (* y y))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'This principle&mdash;that the meaning of a procedure should be independent of the parameter names used by its author&mdash;has the consequence that the parameter names of a procedure must be local to the body of the procedure. Such a name is called a <em>bound variable</em>, and we say that the procedure definition <em>binds</em> its formal parameters. If a variable is not bound, we say that it is <em>free</em>. The set of expressions for which a binding defines a name is called the <em>scope</em> of that name.' },
  { t: 'subsub', title: 'Internal definitions and block structure' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We have one kind of name isolation available to us so far: the formal parameters of a procedure are local to the body of the procedure. The square-root program illustrates another way we would like to control the use of names. The existing program consists of separate procedures&mdash;<code>good-enough?</code>, <code>improve</code>, <code>sqrt-iter</code>&mdash;only one of which is really important to users of the program, namely <code>sqrt</code>. The others only clutter up their minds. We would like to localize the subprocedures, hiding them inside <code>sqrt</code> so that <code>sqrt</code> could coexist with other successive approximations, each having its own private <code>good-enough?</code>. To make this possible, we allow a procedure to have internal definitions that are local to that procedure. Such nesting of definitions is called <em>block structure</em>:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (sqrt x)\n  (define (square a) (* a a))\n  (define (abs a) (if (< a 0) (- a) a))\n  (define (good-enough? guess)\n    (< (abs (- (square guess) x)) 0.001))\n  (define (improve guess)\n    (average guess (/ x guess)))\n  (define (average a b) (/ (+ a b) 2))\n  (define (sqrt-iter guess)\n    (if (good-enough? guess)\n        guess\n        (sqrt-iter (improve guess))))\n  (sqrt-iter 1.0))\n(sqrt 9)\n(sqrt 2)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'In addition to internalizing the definitions of the auxiliary procedures, we have simplified them. Since <code>x</code> is bound in the definition of <code>sqrt</code>, the procedures <code>good-enough?</code>, <code>improve</code>, and <code>sqrt-iter</code>, which are defined internally to <code>sqrt</code>, are in the scope of <code>x</code>. Thus, it is not necessary to pass <code>x</code> explicitly to each of these procedures. Instead, we allow <code>x</code> to be a free variable in the internal definitions. This discipline is called <em>lexical scoping</em>. We will use block structure extensively to help us break up large programs into tractable pieces.' },

  // ─── Section 1.2 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  { t: 'sec', n: '1.2', title: 'Procedures and the Processes They Generate' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We have now considered the elements of programming: primitive arithmetic operations, combination, and definition. But we do not yet know how to describe the <em>shape</em> of a process. A procedure is a pattern for the <em>local evolution</em> of a computational process: it specifies how each stage of the process is built upon the previous stage. We would like to be able to make global statements about the overall, or <em>global</em>, behavior of a process whose local evolution has been specified by a procedure. This is very difficult in general, but we can at least try to describe some typical patterns of process evolution.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.2.1', title: 'Linear Recursion and Iteration' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We begin by considering the factorial function, defined by n! = n &middot; (n&minus;1) &middot; (n&minus;2) &middots; 3 &middot; 2 &middot; 1. There are many ways to compute factorials. One way makes use of the observation that n! = n &middot; (n&minus;1)! for positive n, with 1! = 1:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (factorial n)\n  (if (= n 1)\n      1\n      (* n (factorial (- n 1)))))\n(factorial 6)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Step through this to watch the <em>chain of deferred multiplications</em> that accumulates as the recursion expands, and then collapses:' },
  { t: 'stepper', code: '(define (factorial n)\n  (if (= n 1)\n      1\n      (* n (factorial (- n 1)))))\n(factorial 5)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The shape of this process&mdash;a chain of deferred operations&mdash;is called a <em>linear recursive process</em>. The interpreter must keep track of a chain of operations to perform later; this information grows linearly with n.' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Now consider a different perspective on computing factorials. We maintain a running product together with a counter. At each step the product becomes <code>counter &times; product</code> and the counter increments. This formulation makes the iteration explicit:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (factorial n)\n  (define (iter product counter)\n    (if (> counter n)\n        product\n        (iter (* counter product)\n              (+ counter 1))))\n  (iter 1 1))\n(factorial 6)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Step through this version and notice the contrast: the state at each step is completely captured by the two variables <code>product</code> and <code>counter</code>. The process does not grow; it is a <em>linear iterative process</em>.' },
  { t: 'stepper', code: '(define (factorial n)\n  (define (iter product counter)\n    (if (> counter n)\n        product\n        (iter (* counter product)\n              (+ counter 1))))\n  (iter 1 1))\n(factorial 4)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The contrast between the two steppers is the point. The first grows, then shrinks; the second stays flat. Both compute the same mathematical function, but the <em>shape of the process</em> is completely different.' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'In the iterative case, the program variables provide a complete description of the state of the process at any point. If we stopped the computation between steps, we would only need to supply the interpreter with the values of <code>product</code>, <code>counter</code>, and <code>n</code> to resume. In the recursive case, there is additional &ldquo;hidden&rdquo; information maintained by the interpreter&mdash;the chain of deferred multiplications&mdash;that is not captured in the variables.' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Do not confuse a <em>recursive process</em> with a <em>recursive procedure</em>. When we describe <code>iter</code> as recursive, we mean only that the procedure definition refers to itself. But the process it generates is iterative: its state is captured in a fixed number of variables, and each step can be performed in constant space. In Scheme, an iterative process runs in constant space even when described by a recursive procedure; this is called <em>tail recursion</em>.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.2.2', title: 'Tree Recursion' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Another common pattern of computation is called <em>tree recursion</em>. Consider computing the Fibonacci numbers, each of which is the sum of the preceding two: 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, &hellip; The definition translates directly into a procedure:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (fib n)\n  (cond ((= n 0) 0)\n        ((= n 1) 1)\n        (else (+ (fib (- n 1))\n                 (fib (- n 2))))))\n(fib 10)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Step through a small case to see the tree unfold:' },
  { t: 'stepper', code: '(define (fib n)\n  (cond ((= n 0) 0)\n        ((= n 1) 1)\n        (else (+ (fib (- n 1))\n                 (fib (- n 2))))))\n(fib 4)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Notice that the computation looks like a tree: at each level the branch splits in two. This means the process uses a number of steps that grows <em>exponentially</em> with n. In fact the number of times <code>(fib 1)</code> or <code>(fib 0)</code> is invoked equals Fib(n+1) itself&mdash;the procedure computes redundant values many times. Fib(30) requires over a million calls.' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We can also formulate an iterative process for computing Fibonacci numbers by keeping track of two consecutive values, a = Fib(n+1) and b = Fib(n), and applying the transformation a &larr; a + b, b &larr; a repeatedly:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (fib n)\n  (define (iter a b count)\n    (if (= count 0)\n        b\n        (iter (+ a b) a (- count 1))))\n  (iter 1 0 n))\n(fib 30)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'This second method is a linear iteration; it uses a number of steps linear in n, not exponential. The tree-recursive version, though inefficient, is instructive: it closely mirrors the mathematical definition, and tree recursion is a natural and powerful tool for operating on hierarchically structured data, which we will encounter later.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.2.3', title: 'Orders of Growth' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The previous examples illustrate that processes can differ considerably in the rates at which they consume computational resources. We use the notation &Theta;(f(n)) to describe the <em>order of growth</em> of a process: if the amount of a resource required grows proportionally to f(n), we say the process has order of growth &Theta;(f(n)).' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The linear recursive factorial uses &Theta;(n) steps and &Theta;(n) space (for the deferred chain). The linear iterative factorial uses &Theta;(n) steps but only &Theta;(1) space: a fixed number of variables regardless of n. The tree-recursive Fibonacci uses &Theta;(&phi;<sup>n</sup>) steps (exponential) and &Theta;(n) space (the depth of the tree). Orders of growth give only a rough description of behavior; a process with &Theta;(n&sup2;) steps will take four times as long on 2n inputs, which is often the information that matters.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.2.4', title: 'Exponentiation' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Consider the problem of computing the exponential of a given number. The straightforward recursive definition, based on b<sup>n</sup> = b &middot; b<sup>n&minus;1</sup> and b<sup>0</sup> = 1, requires &Theta;(n) steps and &Theta;(n) space:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (expt b n)\n  (if (= n 0)\n      1\n      (* b (expt b (- n 1)))))\n(expt 2 10)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'A linear iterative version uses &Theta;(n) steps and &Theta;(1) space:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (expt b n)\n  (define (iter b counter product)\n    (if (= counter 0)\n        product\n        (iter b (- counter 1) (* b product))))\n  (iter b n 1))\n(expt 2 10)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We can compute exponentials in fewer steps by using <em>successive squaring</em>. If n is even, b<sup>n</sup> = (b<sup>n/2</sup>)<sup>2</sup>; if n is odd, b<sup>n</sup> = b &middot; b<sup>n&minus;1</sup>. This gives a process that grows as &Theta;(log n):' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (fast-expt b n)\n  (cond ((= n 0) 1)\n        ((even? n) (square (fast-expt b (/ n 2))))\n        (else (* b (fast-expt b (- n 1))))))\n(define (square x) (* x x))\n(fast-expt 2 10)\n(fast-expt 3 20)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Step through a small example to watch the recursion halve the exponent at each even step:' },
  { t: 'stepper', code: '(define (square x) (* x x))\n(define (even? n) (= (remainder n 2) 0))\n(define (fast-expt b n)\n  (cond ((= n 0) 1)\n        ((even? n) (square (fast-expt b (/ n 2))))\n        (else (* b (fast-expt b (- n 1))))))\n(fast-expt 2 8)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The difference between &Theta;(log n) and &Theta;(n) becomes dramatic for large n. To compute 2<sup>1000</sup>, the fast method takes about 10 squarings, while the linear method takes 1000 multiplications.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.2.5', title: 'Greatest Common Divisors' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The greatest common divisor (GCD) of two integers a and b is the largest integer that divides both a and b without a remainder. For example, GCD(16, 28) = 4. An efficient algorithm for computing GCDs is based on the observation that, if r is the remainder when a is divided by b, then the common divisors of a and b are precisely the common divisors of b and r. Thus GCD(a, b) = GCD(b, r):' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (gcd a b)\n  (if (= b 0)\n      a\n      (gcd b (remainder a b))))\n(gcd 206 40)\n(gcd 16 28)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'This is <em>Euclid&rsquo;s Algorithm</em>, one of the oldest known algorithms. Step through it to watch the pair (a, b) shrink toward zero:' },
  { t: 'stepper', code: '(define (gcd a b)\n  (if (= b 0)\n      a\n      (gcd b (remainder a b))))\n(gcd 206 40)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The process generated is iterative (tail-recursive) and the number of steps grows as &Theta;(log n). This fact is known as <em>Lam&eacute;&rsquo;s theorem</em>: the number of steps needed by Euclid&rsquo;s algorithm is never more than five times the number of digits in the smaller of the two inputs.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.2.6', title: 'Example: Testing for Primality' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'This section describes two methods for checking the primality of an integer n, one with order of growth &Theta;(&radic;n) and a &ldquo;probabilistic&rdquo; algorithm with order of growth &Theta;(log n).' },
  { t: 'subsub', title: 'Searching for divisors' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The straightforward way to test primality is to find the number&rsquo;s divisors. A number n is prime if and only if n is its own smallest divisor (greater than 1). The key observation: if d is a divisor of n, so is n/d. Therefore the smallest divisor of n (if n is composite) is at most &radic;n, so we need only test divisors up to &radic;n:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (smallest-divisor n) (find-divisor n 2))\n(define (find-divisor n test-divisor)\n  (cond ((> (square test-divisor) n) n)\n        ((divides? test-divisor n) test-divisor)\n        (else (find-divisor n (+ test-divisor 1)))))\n(define (divides? a b) (= (remainder b a) 0))\n(define (square x) (* x x))\n(define (prime? n) (= n (smallest-divisor n)))\n(prime? 7)\n(prime? 199)\n(prime? 100)' },
  { t: 'subsub', title: 'The Fermat test' },
  { t: 'p', html: '<em>Fermat&rsquo;s Little Theorem</em> states: if n is prime and a is any positive integer less than n, then a<sup>n</sup> is congruent to a modulo n (i.e., a<sup>n</sup> mod n = a). If n is not prime, most integers a will not satisfy this relation. This gives a probabilistic test: pick a random a &lt; n and check whether a<sup>n</sup> &equiv; a (mod n). The <code>expmod</code> procedure computes the exponential of a number modulo another number, using successive squaring for efficiency:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (expmod base exp m)\n  (cond ((= exp 0) 1)\n        ((even? exp)\n         (remainder (square (expmod base (/ exp 2) m)) m))\n        (else\n         (remainder (* base (expmod base (- exp 1) m)) m))))\n(define (square x) (* x x))\n(define (even? n) (= (remainder n 2) 0))\n(define (fermat-test n)\n  (define (try-it a)\n    (= (expmod a n n) a))\n  (try-it (+ 1 (remainder (random-integer 100) (- n 1)))))\n(define (fast-prime? n times)\n  (cond ((= times 0) #t)\n        ((fermat-test n) (fast-prime? n (- times 1)))\n        (else #f)))\n(fast-prime? 199 5)\n(fast-prime? 200 5)\n(fast-prime? 1000003 20)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The Fermat test is a <em>probabilistic</em> algorithm: it cannot guarantee correctness, but the probability of a false positive can be made arbitrarily small by performing more tests. Numbers that fool the Fermat test are called Carmichael numbers; they are exceedingly rare. For most purposes, running the test 20 times gives sufficient confidence.' },

  // ─── Section 1.3 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  { t: 'sec', n: '1.3', title: 'Formulating Abstractions with Higher-Order Procedures' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We have seen that procedures are, in effect, abstractions that describe compound operations on numbers independent of the particular numbers involved. For example, when we define:' },
  { t: 'code', code: '(define (cube x) (* x x x))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'we are not talking about the cube of any particular number, but about a method for obtaining the cube of any number. Of course we could get along without ever defining this procedure, always writing <code>(* 3 3 3)</code>, <code>(* x x x)</code>, etc. directly. But our ability to name procedures and think of them as abstractions is a crucial element of the language. Even more expressive power arises when procedures themselves can be arguments to, or returned as the values of, other procedures. Procedures that manipulate procedures are called <em>higher-order procedures</em>.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.3.1', title: 'Procedures as Arguments' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Consider the following three procedures. The first computes the sum of the integers from a through b; the second computes the sum of the cubes of the integers in that range; the third computes the sum of a sequence of terms in the series 1/(1&middot;3) + 1/(5&middot;7) + 1/(9&middot;11) + &hellip; (which converges to &pi;/8):' },
  { t: 'code', code: '(define (sum-integers a b)\n  (if (> a b)\n      0\n      (+ a (sum-integers (+ a 1) b))))\n\n(define (sum-cubes a b)\n  (if (> a b)\n      0\n      (+ (cube a) (sum-cubes (+ a 1) b))))\n\n(define (pi-sum a b)\n  (if (> a b)\n      0\n      (+ (/ 1.0 (* a (+ a 2))) (pi-sum (+ a 4) b))))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'These three procedures share a common underlying pattern: they are all instances of <em>summation of a series</em> &sum;<sub>n=a</sub><sup>b</sup> f(n). The only differences are the name of the procedure, the function f and the increment from one term to the next. We can factor out the pattern into a single <code>sum</code> procedure that takes the term function and the next-step function as arguments:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (sum term a next b)\n  (if (> a b)\n      0\n      (+ (term a)\n         (sum term (next a) next b))))\n(define (cube x) (* x x x))\n(define (identity x) x)\n(define (inc x) (+ x 1))\n(define (sum-cubes a b) (sum cube a inc b))\n(define (sum-integers a b) (sum identity a inc b))\n(sum-cubes 1 10)\n(sum-integers 1 100)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We can also use <code>sum</code> to formulate the &pi; approximation and numerical integration. Here we pass a procedure that computes each term, and a procedure that steps to the next term:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (sum term a next b)\n  (if (> a b) 0\n      (+ (term a) (sum term (next a) next b))))\n(define (pi-sum a b)\n  (define (pi-term x) (/ 1.0 (* x (+ x 2))))\n  (define (pi-next x) (+ x 4))\n  (sum pi-term a pi-next b))\n(* 8 (pi-sum 1 1000))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Numerical integration (the definite integral of f between a and b) can also be formulated directly with <code>sum</code>: use Simpson&rsquo;s rule with step dx, summing f(a), f(a+dx), f(a+2dx), &hellip;' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (sum term a next b)\n  (if (> a b) 0\n      (+ (term a) (sum term (next a) next b))))\n(define (integral f a b dx)\n  (define (add-dx x) (+ x dx))\n  (* (sum f (+ a (/ dx 2.0)) add-dx b) dx))\n(define (cube x) (* x x x))\n(integral cube 0 1 0.01)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The integral of cube from 0 to 1 is 1/4, so the result should be close to 0.25.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.3.2', title: 'Constructing Procedures Using <code>lambda</code>' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'In section 1.3.1 we defined <code>pi-term</code> and <code>pi-next</code> as named procedures just in order to pass them as arguments. It is inconvenient to define trivial procedures like these and give them names. Instead, we can use the special form <code>lambda</code> to create the procedure directly in place:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (sum term a next b)\n  (if (> a b) 0\n      (+ (term a) (sum term (next a) next b))))\n(define (pi-sum a b)\n  (sum (lambda (x) (/ 1.0 (* x (+ x 2))))\n       a\n       (lambda (x) (+ x 4))\n       b))\n(* 8 (pi-sum 1 1000))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The general form of a lambda expression is <code>(lambda (&#10216;formal parameters&#10217;) &#10216;body&#10217;)</code>. It is the same as <code>define</code> except there is no name. In fact:' },
  { t: 'code', code: '(define (plus4 x) (+ x 4))\n; is equivalent to:\n(define plus4 (lambda (x) (+ x 4)))' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We can read a lambda expression as: &ldquo;the procedure of one argument x that returns &hellip;&rdquo; Lambda is useful anywhere a procedure object is needed, not just as an argument.' },
  { t: 'subsub', title: 'Using <code>let</code> to create local variables' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Another use of lambda is to create local variables. Consider f(x, y) = x(1+xy)<sup>2</sup> + y(1&minus;y) + (1+xy)(1&minus;y). We could write this directly but with repeated subexpressions. Better: let a = 1+xy and b = 1&minus;y, so f = xa<sup>2</sup> + yb + ab. The <code>let</code> form expresses this directly:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (f x y)\n  (let ((a (+ 1 (* x y)))\n        (b (- 1 y)))\n    (+ (* x (square a))\n       (* y b)\n       (* a b))))\n(define (square z) (* z z))\n(f 2 3)' },
  { t: 'p', html: '<code>let</code> is syntactic sugar for a lambda applied immediately: <code>(let ((v e) &hellip;) body)</code> is equivalent to <code>((lambda (v &hellip;) body) e &hellip;)</code>. The binding expressions in a <code>let</code> are evaluated in the <em>outer</em> environment, so a binding cannot refer to another binding in the same <code>let</code>. For sequential bindings, use nested <code>let</code> or internal <code>define</code>.' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.3.3', title: 'Procedures as General Methods' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We introduced compound procedures as a mechanism for abstracting patterns of numerical operations so as to make them independent of the particular numbers involved. With higher-order procedures, we can abstract over computational methods themselves. In this section we discuss two examples: finding zeros of functions by the half-interval method and finding fixed points of functions.' },
  { t: 'subsub', title: 'Finding roots by the half-interval method' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The <em>half-interval method</em> is a simple but powerful technique for finding roots of an equation f(x) = 0. Given f(a) &lt; 0 &lt; f(b), the root lies between a and b. Evaluate f at the midpoint x = (a+b)/2. If f(x) &gt; 0 the root is in [a, x]; if f(x) &lt; 0 it is in [x, b]. Repeat until the interval is small enough:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (search f neg-point pos-point)\n  (let ((midpoint (average neg-point pos-point)))\n    (if (close-enough? neg-point pos-point)\n        midpoint\n        (let ((test-value (f midpoint)))\n          (cond ((positive? test-value) (search f neg-point midpoint))\n                ((negative? test-value) (search f midpoint pos-point))\n                (else midpoint))))))\n(define (average a b) (/ (+ a b) 2))\n(define (close-enough? x y) (< (abs (- x y)) 0.001))\n(define (half-interval-method f a b)\n  (let ((a-value (f a))\n        (b-value (f b)))\n    (cond ((and (negative? a-value) (positive? b-value)) (search f a b))\n          ((and (positive? a-value) (negative? b-value)) (search f b a))\n          (else (error "Values are not of opposite sign")))))\n(half-interval-method sin 2.0 4.0)\n(half-interval-method (lambda (x) (- (* x x x) (* 2 x) 3)) 1.0 2.0)' },
  { t: 'subsub', title: 'Finding fixed points of functions' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'A <em>fixed point</em> of a function f is a value x such that f(x) = x. For some functions, starting from an initial guess and repeatedly applying f will converge to a fixed point. The <code>fixed-point</code> procedure captures this idea:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define tolerance 0.00001)\n(define (fixed-point f first-guess)\n  (define (close-enough? v1 v2)\n    (< (abs (- v1 v2)) tolerance))\n  (define (try guess)\n    (let ((next (f guess)))\n      (if (close-enough? guess next)\n          next\n          (try next))))\n  (try first-guess))\n(fixed-point cos 1.0)\n(fixed-point (lambda (y) (+ (sin y) (cos y))) 1.0)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Computing square roots can be formulated as finding a fixed point: &radic;x is a fixed point of y &mapsto; x/y. But naive iteration oscillates rather than converging. The solution is <em>average damping</em>: instead of applying f directly, average the current and next guess. Here we compute it directly in the fixed-point call:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define tolerance 0.00001)\n(define (fixed-point f first-guess)\n  (define (close-enough? v1 v2) (< (abs (- v1 v2)) tolerance))\n  (define (try guess)\n    (let ((next (f guess)))\n      (if (close-enough? guess next) next (try next))))\n  (try first-guess))\n(define (average a b) (/ (+ a b) 2))\n(define (sqrt x)\n  (fixed-point (lambda (y) (average y (/ x y))) 1.0))\n(sqrt 2.0)\n(sqrt 9.0)' },

  { t: 'sub', n: '1.3.4', title: 'Procedures as Returned Values' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'We can achieve still more expressive power by creating procedures whose returned values are themselves procedures. Consider average damping as a technique: given any function f, the average-damped version maps x to (x + f(x)) / 2. We can express this directly as a procedure that <em>returns</em> a procedure:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define (average-damp f)\n  (lambda (x) (average x (f x))))\n(define (average a b) (/ (+ a b) 2))\n(define (square x) (* x x))\n((average-damp square) 10)\n; Returns (10 + 100) / 2 = 55' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Using <code>average-damp</code> and <code>fixed-point</code>, we can reformulate the square-root procedure:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define tolerance 0.00001)\n(define (fixed-point f first-guess)\n  (define (close-enough? v1 v2) (< (abs (- v1 v2)) tolerance))\n  (define (try guess)\n    (let ((next (f guess)))\n      (if (close-enough? guess next) next (try next))))\n  (try first-guess))\n(define (average a b) (/ (+ a b) 2))\n(define (average-damp f) (lambda (x) (average x (f x))))\n(define (sqrt x)\n  (fixed-point (average-damp (lambda (y) (/ x y))) 1.0))\n(sqrt 2.0)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Cube root is &alpha; fixed point of y &mapsto; x/y<sup>2</sup>; one application of average damping also suffices:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define tolerance 0.00001)\n(define (fixed-point f first-guess)\n  (define (close-enough? v1 v2) (< (abs (- v1 v2)) tolerance))\n  (define (try guess)\n    (let ((next (f guess)))\n      (if (close-enough? guess next) next (try next))))\n  (try first-guess))\n(define (average a b) (/ (+ a b) 2))\n(define (average-damp f) (lambda (x) (average x (f x))))\n(define (square x) (* x x))\n(define (cube-root x)\n  (fixed-point (average-damp (lambda (y) (/ x (square y)))) 1.0))\n(cube-root 8.0)\n(cube-root 27.0)' },
  { t: 'subsub', title: "Newton's method" },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Newton&rsquo;s method is another powerful technique for finding roots: if g is a differentiable function, a root of g can be found by the fixed-point iteration x &mapsto; x &minus; g(x)/g&prime;(x). The derivative of g can be approximated numerically as g&prime;(x) &asymp; (g(x + dx) &minus; g(x)) / dx for small dx. Expressing this as procedures:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define dx 0.00001)\n(define (deriv g)\n  (lambda (x) (/ (- (g (+ x dx)) (g x)) dx)))\n(define (square x) (* x x))\n(define (cube x) (* x x x))\n((deriv cube) 5)   ; derivative of x^3 at 5 is 3*25 = 75' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define tolerance 0.00001)\n(define dx 0.00001)\n(define (fixed-point f first-guess)\n  (define (close-enough? v1 v2) (< (abs (- v1 v2)) tolerance))\n  (define (try guess)\n    (let ((next (f guess)))\n      (if (close-enough? guess next) next (try next))))\n  (try first-guess))\n(define (deriv g)\n  (lambda (x) (/ (- (g (+ x dx)) (g x)) dx)))\n(define (newton-transform g)\n  (lambda (x) (- x (/ (g x) ((deriv g) x)))))\n(define (newtons-method g guess)\n  (fixed-point (newton-transform g) guess))\n(define (sqrt x)\n  (newtons-method (lambda (y) (- (square y) x)) 1.0))\n(define (square x) (* x x))\n(sqrt 2.0)' },
  { t: 'subsub', title: 'Abstractions and first-class procedures' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'Both the average-damp method and Newton&rsquo;s method start from a function and compute a fixed point of a derived function. We can abstract this into a single <em>fixed-point transform</em> procedure:' },
  { t: 'code', live: true, code: '(define tolerance 0.00001)\n(define dx 0.00001)\n(define (fixed-point f first-guess)\n  (define (close-enough? v1 v2) (< (abs (- v1 v2)) tolerance))\n  (define (try guess)\n    (let ((next (f guess)))\n      (if (close-enough? guess next) next (try next))))\n  (try first-guess))\n(define (average a b) (/ (+ a b) 2))\n(define (average-damp f) (lambda (x) (average x (f x))))\n(define (deriv g) (lambda (x) (/ (- (g (+ dx x)) (g x)) dx)))\n(define (newton-transform g) (lambda (x) (- x (/ (g x) ((deriv g) x)))))\n(define (fixed-point-of-transform g transform guess)\n  (fixed-point (transform g) guess))\n(define (square x) (* x x))\n(define (sqrt-v1 x)\n  (fixed-point-of-transform (lambda (y) (/ x y)) average-damp 1.0))\n(define (sqrt-v2 x)\n  (fixed-point-of-transform (lambda (y) (- (square y) x)) newton-transform 1.0))\n(sqrt-v1 2.0)\n(sqrt-v2 2.0)' },
  { t: 'p', html: 'The significance of higher-order procedures is that they allow us to represent these abstractions explicitly as elements of our programming language, so that they can be handled just like other computational elements. In general, programming languages impose restrictions on the ways in which computational elements can be manipulated. Elements with the fewest restrictions are said to have <em>first-class</em> status. Some of the rights and privileges of first-class elements are: they may be named by variables; they may be passed as arguments to procedures; they may be returned as the results of procedures; they may be included in data structures. Scheme, unlike many other languages, awards procedures full first-class status. This poses challenges for efficient implementation, but the resulting gain in expressive power is enormous.' },
];
